<section id="monday">
  <div class="section-title">
    <div class="eyebrow">Part 02 · Monday Practice</div>
    <h2>Git and GitHub Exercises</h2>
    <p>
      These exercises helped me understand how Git tracks changes
      and how developers organize their work using repositories
      and branches.
    </p>
  </div>

  <article class="card exercise">
    <div class="exercise-label">E1 · FIRST REPOSITORY</div>
    <h3>Create Your First Git Repository</h3>
    <p><strong>Goal:</strong> Initialize a Git repository, create a file,
      check its status, and make the first commit.</p>
    <p>
      I checked the Git version and initialized a repository
      with the main branch. I created index.html, staged it,
      and committed the file.
    </p>
    <pre><code>git --version
git init -b main
git status
git add index.html
git commit -m "Initial commit: add index.html"
git log --oneline</code></pre>
    <p><strong>Actual commit from my exercise:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>a5df21e Initial commit: add index.html</code></pre>
    <div class="result">
      Result: Git tracked index.html after it was staged,
      and I created my first commit.
    </div>
  </article>

  <article class="card exercise">
    <div class="exercise-label">E2 · BRANCH AND MERGE</div>
    <h3>Create a Branch and Merge It</h3>
    <p><strong>Goal:</strong> Create a feature branch, make a change,
      and merge it back into main.</p>
    <p>
      I created a branch called feature-style and added a
      stylesheet. After committing the change, I switched
      back to main and merged the feature branch.
    </p>
    <pre><code>git checkout -b feature-style
git add style.css
git commit -m "Add basic stylesheet"
git checkout main
git merge feature-style
git log --oneline --graph --all</code></pre>
    <p><strong>Actual stylesheet commit:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>df1e4f9 Add basic stylesheet</code></pre>
    <div class="result">
      Result: The feature-style branch was merged into main
      using a fast-forward merge.
    </div>
  </article>

  <article class="card exercise">
    <div class="exercise-label">E3 · PUSH TO GITHUB</div>
    <h3>Push a Local Repository to GitHub</h3>
    <p><strong>Goal:</strong> Connect a local Git repository
      to a remote GitHub repository and upload its commits.</p>
    <p>
      After creating an empty repository on GitHub, the local
      repository can be connected to it using a remote named origin.
    </p>
    <pre><code>git remote add origin YOUR_REPOSITORY_URL
git remote -v
git push -u origin main</code></pre>
    <div class="result">
      Expected result: The local main branch is uploaded to
      GitHub and tracks origin/main.
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      My previously shared terminal evidence confirms the local
      commits in E1 and E2. I still need to attach the actual
      remote push evidence and repository link for this exercise.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="card exercise">
    <div class="exercise-label">EXTRA · PULL REQUEST</div>
    <h3>Create and Merge a Pull Request</h3>
    <p><strong>Goal:</strong> Practise the GitHub collaboration
      workflow by creating a branch, pushing it, opening a pull
      request, and merging it.</p>
    <pre><code>git checkout -b add-readme
git add README.md
git commit -m "Add README describing the exercise"
git push -u origin add-readme</code></pre>
    <p>
      After pushing the branch, a pull request can be opened
      on GitHub to propose merging the changes into main.
      The pull request can then be reviewed and merged.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      The pull request and merge still need to be completed
      and linked here. These commands describe the exercise,
      not a claim that a pull request has already been merged.
    </p>
  </article>
